 "poem": "The damsel of the Isaurian elevation\nShee dropt her eyes, and fell with soft dismay;\nAnd she cried aloud: \"Was it anyone\nWho struck thee?\" She said. - The dame yesterday\nUnto herself did girdle'd admiration.\nNow in all that knelt beneath herself\nThere was no one but 'the damsel' one.\nAs a son's cry upon his parents\nI answered (and such cries must arise,\nIn fatal dress) - \"She hath been astray,\nOne oh! For us both!\" - \"Ah, but ye realize\nThat not the moment shall be away,",
